WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.967
Object detection: IoU, NMS and mAP.

2
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:09.533
Models come and go, but these three concepts judge and clean up all of them.

3
00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:13.281
Detection means localize and classify.

4
00:00:13.281 --> 00:00:16.633
First we need to measure how well two boxes agree.

5
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:18.919
That's intersection over union.

6
00:00:18.919 --> 00:00:21.033
On top, the area the boxes share.

7
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:23.600
Underneath, the combined area they cover.

8
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.689
Zero means disjoint, one means identical.

9
00:00:26.689 --> 00:00:33.967
A prediction counts as correct when its IoU with the truth clears a threshold, classically zero point five.

10
00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:39.170
Every detector emits near duplicate boxes around each object.

11
00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.467
Non max suppression cleans them up.

12
00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:43.400
Keep the highest scoring box.

13
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.200
Delete its neighbours whose IoU with it exceeds a threshold.

14
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.433
Then repeat on whatever remains.

15
00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:53.433
Now the headline score, mean average precision.

16
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:55.500
Sweep the confidence threshold.

17
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.700
That traces a precision recall curve for each class.

18
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.900
The area under that curve is the average precision.

19
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.900
Average over classes to get mAP.

20
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:10.300
The stricter COCO version also averages over ten IoU thresholds.

21
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.100
Here is IoU in Python.

22
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:19.200
We find the corners of the overlap rectangle, and its area is zero if the boxes are disjoint.

23
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.600
Then divide by the combined area.

24
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.553
A small box fully inside a big one scores only zero point three six.

25
00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:28.400
Containment is not a match.

26
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:30.867
A real bug story.

27
00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:35.333
A dashboard reports twenty three ships in a scene that plainly holds nine.

28
00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:43.467
The tiles overlap by twenty percent, so ships near a seam are detected twice, and per tile NMS can't see across tiles.

29
00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:49.303
The fix: merge all boxes into scene coordinates and run one global NMS pass.

30
00:01:49.303 --> 00:01:50.833
No retraining needed.

31
00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:52.889
Let's recap.

32
00:01:52.889 --> 00:01:55.600
IoU is shared area over combined area.

33
00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.967
NMS keeps the best box and deletes duplicates.

34
00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:02.900
mAP averages precision over classes.

35
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.933
And much of detection hygiene lives outside the network.

36
00:02:06.933 --> 00:02:08.800
Next up: image segmentation.
